#: app/views/about.scm:174 app/views/about.scm:184
msgid ""
"the account of a build step: where this console came from, the framework it "
"stands on, and why it was written to be extended rather than configured."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:183 app/views/about.scm:193
msgid "the short version"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:185 app/views/about.scm:195
msgid ""
"byggsteg is the Norwegian word for a build step, and that is the size of the "
"thing: one step, then the next, and then the one nobody has written yet. It "
"is a continuous integration and delivery system written in Lisp, and it is "
"made of five moving parts you can read, run and replace."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:188 app/views/about.scm:198
msgid ""
"a piece of Lisp that returns a plan: the project, the branch to take, where "
"to clone it from and which tasks to run on it"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:191 app/views/about.scm:201
msgid ""
"one loop in one thread, taking whatever the queue holds and doing the steps "
"the job asks for"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:194 app/views/about.scm:204
msgid ""
"written as the command prints it, so a run is read while it runs rather than "
"once it is over"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:197 app/views/about.scm:207
msgid ""
"what a run leaves behind: kept where the deployment can serve it, and swept "
"when it is no longer wanted"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:200 app/views/about.scm:210
msgid ""
"one SQLite file and one server on a machine of your own, under a licence "
"that lets you take it apart"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:206 app/views/about.scm:216
msgid "how byggsteg came to be"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:208 app/views/about.scm:218
msgid ""
"There is no first version of this page. Every delivery system is built "
"twice: once by the people who had to deliver before it existed, and once "
"more by whoever inherits the story. This is the second telling of "
"byggsteg's, and it is shorter than the first."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:210 app/views/about.scm:220
msgid ""
"Before byggsteg, a release was a ritual performed by hand. A person signed "
"in to a machine, pulled a branch, built it, restarted a service, and "
"watched. It worked. It always works, for one person, on one machine, at the "
"hour that person happens to be awake. What it could not do was remember what "
"it had done, and what it could not say was why it had failed."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:212 app/views/about.scm:222
msgid ""
"Then two people met, and a door appeared where a wall had stood. Mu Lei, "
"known as NalaGinrut, showed Josep Bigorra, known as jjba23, GNU Artanis, and "
"with the framework came a whole new world of possibilities: pages that are "
"lists, routes that are procedure calls, and a place to put everything a "
"deployment believes about itself. This console is what grew in that world."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:214 app/views/about.scm:224
msgid ""
"The framework itself came out of a question. At GNU, the project was "
"choosing a language for its own website, and many of the answers were "
"Python. That struck NalaGinrut as strange, because the official extension "
"language of GNU is Guile; so he asked instead why nobody had begun a project "
"to write the web in it. RMS agreed it was a good idea. For a while it was "
"only an idea, without a plan. Then the Guile community held a hack-potluck "
"to celebrate Guile 2 turning two — a contest to write something cool in a "
"few weeks — and the framework was born there, in February 2013. It was "
"entered in the Lisp in Summer Projects contest and certified an awesome "
"project; the first stable release arrived on the first of January, 2015; and "
"on the nineteenth of that month it was offered to the FSF, where RMS "
"inducted it as an official GNU project. That is the ground this console "
"stands on."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:216 app/views/about.scm:226
msgid ""
"And so byggsteg was written in that world and named in another. It is a "
"build step, and only a build step: not a platform, not a shelf, not a "
"product with a feature list. The smallest thing that can be asked of a "
"delivery is the thing it was built to answer."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:222 app/views/about.scm:232
msgid "the world that Artanis opened"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:224 app/views/about.scm:234
msgid ""
"It is worth saying plainly what was handed over, because it was not a "
"library so much as a country with its borders left open."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:227 app/views/about.scm:237
msgid ""
"a page is announced as a method and a path, and the answer is a procedure of "
"your own"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:230 app/views/about.scm:240
msgid ""
"SXML: the page you are reading is a list, built by Scheme, in the same "
"language as the job it describes"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:233 app/views/about.scm:243
msgid ""
"translation is part of the framework, which is why this deployment answers "
"in English, Dutch, Spanish and Japanese"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:236 app/views/about.scm:246
msgid ""
"mime types, cookies, static files and a page of its own for each status "
"code: a frame a deployment hangs its own chrome on"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:239 app/views/about.scm:249
msgid ""
"the whole console is one process started from one configuration file, with "
"no container between you and it"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:241 app/views/about.scm:251
msgid ""
"Of all of it, one sentence did the most work: a page in Artanis is a list, "
"and a list is a program. A system whose interface is a list is a system "
"whose interface can be computed, and a system whose interface can be "
"computed is one nobody has to wait for permission to change."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:262 app/views/about.scm:272
msgid "an infinitely extensible system"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:264 app/views/about.scm:274
msgid ""
"Most continuous integration systems are shelves. You take a step down from "
"the shelf, put it in the order the shelf expects, and configure it. A shelf "
"is useful, and a shelf is also a ceiling: what it cannot hold, you cannot do."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:266 app/views/about.scm:276
msgid ""
"byggsteg was built the other way round — it is a library with a page on top "
"— and the library has three doors in it, one for each thing a delivery is "
"made of."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:269 app/views/about.scm:279
msgid ""
"a thunk returning an alist, so a pipeline is a value your own program can "
"compute, save as a profile, or send from a phone"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:272 app/views/about.scm:282
msgid ""
"the worker looks the name up and runs the clause that answers to it; "
"teaching byggsteg a new verb is adding a clause, not inventing a plugin "
"protocol"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:275 app/views/about.scm:285
msgid ""
"it is handed the clone directory, announces what it is doing in the log as "
"it does it, and answers with a result — and the trivial task is the door "
"left open for the day a deployment wants something nobody has written yet"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:278 app/views/about.scm:288
msgid ""
"Nothing in it is finished in the sense of closed. The set of things byggsteg "
"can do is not a list of features somebody chose in advance; it is the set of "
"things the person running it can write down. That is what an infinitely "
"extensible CI/CD system means — not a system with infinite features, but a "
"system whose next feature is a definition."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:284 app/views/about.scm:294
msgid "who made what"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:286 app/views/about.scm:296
msgid ""
"Every file of byggsteg carries two copyright lines, and they are the story "
"told plainly: the orchestrator and the framework it is served from, the "
"maintainer who wrote one and the engineer who wrote the other."
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:289 app/views/about.scm:299
msgid ""
"Josep Bigorra (@jjba23): the orchestrator, its pages, and hygguile, the "
"interface library they are built from"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:292 app/views/about.scm:302
msgid ""
"Mu Lei (@NalaGinrut): the framework every page of this deployment is served "
"from, and the second name on each file of this repository"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:295 app/views/about.scm:305
msgid ""
"the language both of them are written in, and the reason a job can be data"
msgstr ""

#: app/views/about.scm:298 app/views/about.scm:308
msgid ""
"the utility stylesheet the terminal is dressed in, also by @jjba23, under "
